This is a solid, standard foundation. You are correctly setting up the modern React 18 root API and wrapping your app in the Query Provider immediately.

However, for a High-Performance Map Application (where you download heavy 20MB+ GeoJSON files), the default QueryClient settings are dangerous.

By default, React Query treats data as "stale" immediately. If a user alt-tabs away and comes back, React Query will try to re-download your massive map data. You need to tweak this to prevent performance spikes.
The "Pro" Setup for Map Apps

Here is the optimized version. Copy this into your main.tsx:
TypeScript

import { StrictMode } from 'react'
import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client'
import { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/react-query'
import { ReactQueryDevtools } from '@tanstack/react-query-devtools' // 1. Debugging Tool
import './index.css'
import App from './App.tsx'

// 2. Optimized Configuration for Heavy Data
const queryClient = new QueryClient({
  defaultOptions: {
    queries: {
      // Map data doesn't change often. Don't refetch it when window refocuses.
      refetchOnWindowFocus: false,
      // Keep data "fresh" for 5 minutes (prevents spamming your backend)
      staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000,
      // If a request fails, don't retry immediately (saves API costs)
      retry: 1,
    },
  },
})

createRoot(document.getElementById('root')!).render(
  <StrictMode>
    <QueryClientProvider client={queryClient}>
      <App />
      {/* 3. The DevTools toggle button (only shows in dev) */}
      <ReactQueryDevtools initialIsOpen={false} />
    </QueryClientProvider>
  </StrictMode>,
)

Why these changes matter for you:

    refetchOnWindowFocus: false: Imagine a user is looking at your 3D map, switches to Slack, and switches back. With the default setup, your app would try to re-download the buildings. This setting stops that.

    staleTime: 5 mins: Building data (geometries) rarely changes. There is no need to check for updates every second.

    ReactQueryDevtools: This adds a tiny floating icon in development. It lets you see exactly what data is cached and is a lifesaver when debugging why a map layer isn't loading.
